<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Network security policy.

    WHY THIS MATTERS
    ================
    This file used to carry `cleartextTrafficPermitted="true"` on base-config, i.e.
    globally. That is a platform-level opt-out of TLS enforcement for every outbound
    HTTP connection the app makes. The failure mode is not theoretical: BlackClaw
    ships `Authorization: Bearer <api-key>` headers to the LLM endpoint alongside the
    entire prompt stream (screen OCR, notification text, clipboard contents, SMS),
    and it fires user-configured webhooks. On hostile Wi-Fi, anyone on-path could
    read all of it and rewrite the responses — and rewriting an LLM response means
    choosing which tools the agent invokes on the device. So a downgrade here is not
    a confidentiality bug only, it is remote control.

    Denying cleartext by default means the platform rejects the connection before a
    single byte leaves, independently of whatever the app-layer validators do or
    forget to do. That is the point: it is the backstop for the paths nobody
    validated.

    LOOPBACK EXEMPTION
    ==================
    127.0.0.1 / ::1 / localhost stay permitted because two things genuinely live
    there and neither serves TLS:
      - the embedded config server (server/ConfigServer.kt binds 127.0.0.1)
      - on-device model runtimes (llama.cpp / ollama style servers on the phone)
    Traffic that never leaves the device cannot be intercepted on the network, so
    there is nothing for TLS to protect against in that hop.

    KNOWN REGRESSION — http:// MODEL SERVERS ON THE LAN
    ===================================================
    server/ConfigServerPolicy.isSafeLlmBaseUrl() deliberately accepts http:// to
    RFC1918 / link-local addresses, because pointing BlackClaw at ollama running on
    a desktop (e.g. http://192.168.1.50:11434) is a documented, first-class use
    case. Android's network security config CANNOT express an address range: a
    <domain> entry is a literal hostname or a literal IP, so 192.168.0.0/16 would
    mean enumerating 65k entries. There is no wildcard.

    We accept the break rather than keep the global hole. Consequence, stated
    plainly: with this config a LAN http:// base URL is accepted by the app-layer
    validator and then refused by the platform with
      javax.net.ssl / java.net.UnknownServiceException:
      "CLEARTEXT communication to 192.168.1.50 not permitted by network security policy"
    Users in that situation must either put TLS in front of their model server or
    reach it over loopback (adb reverse / a local tunnel). The app-layer validator
    was left as-is on purpose — it is not ours to weaken, and it still guards the
    https case — but the two layers now disagree about LAN http, and the platform
    layer is the one that wins.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <!-- Default for every destination: TLS required, system CAs only. -->
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>

    <!-- On-device only. Never leaves the handset, so there is no on-path attacker
         for TLS to defend against. Scoped to literal loopback addresses — no
         includeSubdomains, no LAN ranges. -->
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">::1</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">localhost</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
